---
/**
 * /design, lists: the data table and its row delegation, the library rows
 * with month headers and a private row, the tag filter, the post wall and the
 * palette's empty state. Public entries plus one synthetic private row.
 */
import "../../styles/table.css";
import LibraryRows from "../LibraryRows.astro";
import LibraryTags from "../LibraryTags.astro";
import PostWall from "../PostWall.astro";
import { library, parseLibrary } from "../../lib/library";

/** The newest entry of the two latest months, so two month headers show. */
const months = [...new Set(library.map((entry) => entry.saved_date.slice(0, 7)))].sort().reverse().slice(0, 2);
const rows = months.flatMap((month) => library.filter((entry) => entry.saved_date.startsWith(month)).slice(0, 2));

/** Made up for this page: no private entry is ever in this repo. */
const [locked] = parseLibrary([
  { slug: "example-private-row", title: "A row only I can see", url: "https://example.com/", domain: "example.com", saved_date: rows[0]!.saved_date, kind: "article", note: "Signed-in rows merge in by date, with a lock." },
]);

const posts = library.filter((entry) => entry.post !== null).slice(0, 4);

const TABLE = [
  { name: "Tools", href: "/tools", note: "Software I ran, with a dated verdict" },
  { name: "Sites", href: "/sites", note: "Websites I saved for their design" },
  { name: "Library", href: "/library", note: "Articles, posts and videos I saved" },
];
---

<section>
  <h2 class="sheet__head mono">Lists</h2>
  <p>
    /tools and /sites share one table. The whole row is the target: a press
    anywhere on it opens the row's one link, and the link is the tab stop. No
    stretched <span class="code">::after</span>, because iPadOS doesn't make a
    table row a containing block, so every row's overlay covered the table and
    the last row took every tap.
  </p>
  <table class="dtable specimen">
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Name</th><th scope="col">What</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {TABLE.map((row) => (
        <tr>
          <td><span class="row__name"><a class="row__link" href={row.href}>{row.name}</a></span></td>
          <td>{row.note}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Library rows sit under month headers, each with its count. Signed in, my
    private rows merge in by date and carry a lock. The one below is made up.
  </p>
  <div class="specimen specimen--box">
    <LibraryRows entries={rows} extra={locked ? [locked] : []} />
  </div>
  <p>
    Signed in, the top bar adds "Private" with a count and "Sign out", and my
    rows join the pane and ⌘K. Signed out, a page carries one 110-byte check
    and nothing else. /me is the one page built on request.
  </p>
  <p>
    The sign-in form is Clerk's, restyled with plain CSS on its <span class="code">cl-</span>
    classes: no card, a square 44px field with a hairline, one solid ink button. Clerk loads only on /me, so it isn't drawn
    here.
  </p>

  <p>
    Tags filter the list from a native disclosure. Tick two and you get entries
    with both. On /library each count follows the filter.
  </p>
  <div class="specimen">
    <LibraryTags />
  </div>

  <p>
    Posts are a masonry wall: CSS columns, 4, 3, 2 or 1 by the wall's own width,
    each card lifted by a hairline edge and two soft shadows.
  </p>
  <div class="specimen">
    <PostWall entries={posts} compact />
  </div>

  <p>
    Before you type, ⌘K shows five groups: Go to, Browse, Recent saves, Actions
    and Try searching. The arrows walk across all of them.
  </p>
  <p class="specimen">
    <button type="button" class="ctl press" data-palette-open>Open the palette</button>
  </p>
</section>

<script>
  import "../../lib/row-link";
</script>

<style>
  .specimen {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .specimen--box {
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }

  /* The shipped table bleeds past a full-width shell; on this column it sits flush. */
  .dtable.specimen {
    width: 100%;
    margin-inline: 0;
  }

  .dtable.specimen :global(:is(th, td):first-child) {
    padding-left: 0.75rem;
  }
</style>
